  "image_name": "0cd703c6171f69a2fdbf470a4b98ad89.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how several membrane receptors and transporters are internalized and then sorted to different intracellular destinations. The top schematic shows the general trafficking network: cargo is taken up from the plasma membrane in an endocytic vesicle, enters a sorting endosome, and is then either recycled back to the surface, sent through recycling endosomes or the Golgi/TGN and storage vesicles, or directed onward to late endosomes and lysosomes for degradation. Small labels for clathrin, caveolin, β-arrestin, AP2, dynamin and Rab proteins indicate the machinery controlling these routes.\n\nThe lower panels apply this common map to specific metabolic receptors. The insulin receptor appears to be largely recycled after endocytosis. The glucagon receptor shows a condition-dependent fate: with low glucagon it is recycled, whereas high glucagon promotes movement toward lysosomal degradation. GIPR is shown with altered routing depending on the presence or absence of GIP, suggesting ligand-dependent recycling versus degradation. GLP1R mainly appears to recycle. GLUT4 is depicted cycling between internal compartments, the Golgi/recycling system, storage vesicles, and the surface rather than being degraded. LDLR seems to internalize and recycle efficiently while delivering cargo toward degradative compartments. Overall, the image highlights selective endosomal sorting as a key regulator of receptor availability and signaling.",
